@title Checkout Outage on October 18
@subtitle Incident report: 47 minutes on Sunday morning
@author Incident review team, Customer Platform
@date 2026-10-19

!!! Customers could not complete a purchase on our website or app for 47 minutes, from 10:12 AM to 10:59 AM Eastern on Sunday, October 18. No customer was charged twice and no payment data was exposed.

kpi Outage length | 47 min | longest this year | bad
kpi Failed checkout attempts | 27,900 | 67% of attempts | bad
kpi Orders lost (estimate) | 10,500 | about $672,000 | bad
kpi Customers charged twice | 0 | confirmed by Finance | good

## In plain terms

Every time a customer clicks Pay, our checkout service borrows one of a fixed number of shared lines to the orders database, saves the order, and hands the line back. We normally allow 200 lines at once. A routine settings change on Sunday morning cut that number to 20. Sunday morning is one of our busiest times, so the 20 lines were used up within minutes and everyone else got an error.

The change was meant for our test environment, which has very little traffic. It was copied into the live settings by mistake. Fixing it took 47 minutes because our usual way of undoing a change refused to run while checkout was unhealthy.

diagram flow dir=LR title="What happens when a customer clicks Pay"
flow: cust "Customer" user
flow: site "Website or app" svc
flow: co "Checkout service" svc
flow: pool "Shared database lines" svc "Limit was 200, became 20" focus
flow: db "Orders database" db
flow: cust -> site : clicks Pay
flow: site -> co
flow: co -> pool : needs a line
flow: pool -> db : saves order

